A presidential election was held in Portugal on 13 January 1991. 

Incumbent president [[Mário Soares]] was eliglbe to run for a second consecutive term, and was a candidate for re-election.<ref>{{Cite web |date=1990-06-10 |title=Soares anuncia recandidatura a Belém |url=https://arquivos.rtp.pt/conteudos/soares-anuncia-recandidatura-a-belem/ | website= RTP Arquivos |access-date=2026-08-28 |language=pt-PT}}</ref> Soares was easily re-elected as head of state, achieving more than 70% of the votes, the highest percentage of votes ever recorded for a presidential candidate in Portuguese democracy.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-01-18 |title=Presidenciais. Mário Soares foi o presidente eleito com maior percentagem de votos desde 1976 |url=https://observador.pt/2026/01/18/presidenciais-mario-soares-foi-o-presidente-eleito-com-maior-percentagem-de-votos-desde-1976/ | website= Observador |access-date=2026-08-28 |language=pt-PT}}</ref> Mário Soares gained a majority of the votes in every district of the country, and in 295 out of the then 305 municipalities.

As the election of a center-left candidate was assured, other left-wing parties, the [[Portuguese Communist Party]] and the [[People's Democratic Union (Portugal)|People's Democratic Union]], presented their own candidates. The communists presented [[Carlos Carvalhas]], who had been Assistant General Secretary of the Party a year before ([[Álvaro Cunhal]] was the secretary-general), which polled third with 13% of the votes. Carvalhas would later be elected secretary-general of the party in 1992. Mário Soares achieved the majority of the votes in every district of the country, and 295 of the then 305 municipalities. On the right, the [[Social Democratic Party (Portugal)|Social Democratic Party]] supported Soares, while the [[People's Party (Portugal)|Democratic and Social Centre]] supported the candidacy of [[Basílio Horta]], who finished second with 14% of the votes. [[Voter turnout]] had a significant drop compared with the previous election, with 62% of voters casting a ballot. Soares was sworn in for a second term as President on 9 March 1991.<ref>{{Cite web |date=1991-03-09 |title=Dia da tomada de posse de Mário Soares |url=https://arquivos.rtp.pt/conteudos/dia-da-tomada-de-posse-de-mario-soares/| website= RTP Arquivos |access-date=2026-02-18 |language=pt-PT}}</ref>

==Background==
Mário Soares was elected president, on the second round in February 1986, with 51% of the votes.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-01-14 |title=Mário Soares e Freitas do Amaral: as históricas presidenciais de há 40 anos (1986) |url=https://expresso.pt/podcasts/a-historia-repete-se/2026-01-14-mario-soares-e-freitas-do-amaral-as-historicas-presidenciais-de-ha-40-anos--1986--85bb030a | website= Expresso |access-date=2026-08-26 |language=pt-PT}}</ref> Soares' first term was marked by the calm [[Cohabitation (government)|cohabitation]] with PSD prime minister [[Aníbal Cavaco Silva]], with Soares rejecting an alternative PS-PCP-PRD government after the approval of a motion of no confidence against Cavaco's minority government in April 1987,<ref>{{Cite web |date=2015-10-15 |title=Como Soares e Sampaio chumbaram governos maioritários sem o partido mais votado |url=https://www.sabado.pt/portugal/detalhe/como-soares-e-sampaio-chumbaram-governos-maioritarios-sem-o-partido-mais-votado | website= Sábado |access-date=2026-08-26 |language=pt-PT}}</ref> and calling [[1987 Portuguese legislative election|an election]] that would result in a landslide absolute majority for the PSD.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2007-04-07 |title=1987 – Maioria Absoluta do PSD |url=https://arquivos.rtp.pt/conteudos/1987-maioria-absoluta-do-psd/ | website= RTP |access-date=2026-08-26 |language=pt-PT}}</ref> Major economic and constitutional reforms, along with modernization driven by the arrival of European funds, and strong economic growth, also characterized Soares' first term as head of state.<ref>{{Cite web |title=1988: o ano ‘zero’ das privatizações |url=https://expresso.pt/economia/2023-04-27-1988-o-ano-zero-das-privatizacoes-64895169 |access-date=2026-08-26 | date=2023-04-29 | website=Expresso |language=pt}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=6. As revisões constitucionais (1982-2005) |url=https://50anos25abril.pt/historia/50-anos-da-constituicao-da-republica-portuguesa-1976-2026/6-as-revisoes-constitucionais-1982-2005-mudanca-adaptacao-e-continuidade-democratica/ |access-date=2026-08-26 | website=50 Anos do 25 de Abril |language=pt}}</ref> 

===Pre-campaign===

In the follow-up for the presidential election, divisions on the right-wing emerged, mainly in the [[CDS – People's Party|Democratic and Social Center]] (CDS), regarding a candidate to face Soares. Former party leader [[Francisco Lucas Pires]] considered a run,<ref name=":0" /> but withdrew during the summer of 1990 due to lack of support and Soares' strong popularity,<ref name=":91" /> while others, namely party leader and 1986 candidate, [[Diogo Freitas do Amaral]], rejected a "rematch" against Soares.<ref name=":0" /> In the end, the party gave its support to the candidacy of [[Basílio Horta]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=CDS apoia Basílio Horta  |url=https://arquivos.rtp.pt/conteudos/cds-apoia-basilio-horta/ |access-date=2026-08-28 | date=1990-10-20 | website=RTP |language=pt}}</ref> On the Social Democrats (PSD), the issue of the presidential elections was not seen as very important, despite [[Madeira|Madeiran]] president [[Alberto João Jardim]] proposing that the party abstained from supporting any candidate, being quite critical of Soares and with some even considering his name as a possible candidate.<ref name=":0" /> Former president [[António Ramalho Eanes]], another strong critic of president Soares, also rejected any idea of running again for office,<ref name=":0" /> especially after leading his party, the [[Democratic Renewal Party (Portugal)|Democratic Renewal Party]] (PRD), to a massive defeat in the 1987 legislative election.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Os Presidentes  |url=https://www.publico.pt/2006/01/19/jornal/os-presidentes-59064 |access-date=2026-08-28 | date=2006-01-19 | website=Público |language=pt}}</ref>

==Electoral system==
Any [[Portuguese nationality law|Portuguese citizen]] over 35 years old has the opportunity to run for president. In order to do so it is necessary to gather between 7,500 and 15,000 signatures and submit them to the [[Portuguese Constitutional Court]].

According to the [[Constitution of Portugal|Portuguese Constitution]], to be elected, a candidate needs a majority of votes. If no candidate gets this majority there will take place a second round between the two most voted candidates.

